            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** The main doors. Consider replacing weather stripping



** Around some of the older windows



** Up the stairwell to the basement, suggesting air leakage from a source down there. Likely the header spaces.



** Some exterior electrical outlets. Foam backing plates can be installed behind the face plates where necessary.



Savings: If you address some of the air leakage locations in your home, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $140 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.7 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------







</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

INSULATE MAIN WALLS



The main walls of your home appear to be 2x4 construction with batt insulation (R12). I have modelled adding 4 additional inches (R20) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior (total R32) to bring your building up to the Yellowknife building bylaw standards. Due to the large amount of labour involved in adding insulation to the exterior of your home, and the high cost, this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade.



Alternatively, in order to acheive a similar R-value increase in your walls, you could use spray foam. To acheive approximately R32 you would need to remove the existing vapour barrier and the batt insulation, and add 2 inches of strapping to the interior side of the walls, giving a total wall cavity thickness of 5.5 inches. The added benefit of using spray foam is that it would act as an effective air and vapour barrier if installed correctly. Please note, adding spray foam to the existing 3.5 inch deep wall cavity would only bring your wall to approximately an R20 level, which would not be worth it from a cost savings and effort perspective. Please see the additional information below if you are considering bringing your walls up to R32. 



Savings: By retrofitting the insulation in all exterior walls of your home, you could potentially save $960 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 39 GJ, and qualify for up to a $4,000 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



***If you are planning to add insulation your exterior walls AEA currently offers a new program, called the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019. It can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



In the above modelled example I've provided, using either 4.0 inches of XPS or strapping the interior walls and applying spray foam you would likely qualify for the max rebate of $8,000, assuming you insulate 100% of your exterior walls.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit (this evaluation counts towards that) and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. Please note, this program is only available on a first come, first served basis, and dependent on funding availability at AEA. By acheiving the above insulation requirements for this program it also opens up other rebates for things such as air sealing, windows, etc.





For more information and rebate application forms, please call me or visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation>

INSULATE BASEMENT WALLS



The basement walls in one half of the basement appear to be approximately 9 inches of concrete with likely interior 2x4 walls, whereas in the other half (lower ceiling half) the basement walls appear to be just 9 inches of uninsulated concrete. I have modeled adding walls with R15 insulation to the uninsulated half of the basement.



Savings: By adding framing and R15 of insulation to the (lower ceiling portion) interior basement walls you could potentially save $750 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 30 GJ, and qualify for up to a maximum of $325 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



There may be other funding opportunites available if you enter into the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program outlined previously.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





</foundation>
                <windows>

WINDOWS



Most of the windows in this house are older double paned, wood windows. Some windows are newer vinyl, triple paned windows. I modelled upgrading all the windows to ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 520 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 21 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, but there are rebates availabe in the DHERP.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------







</windows>
                <doors>

DOORS



The main exterior door in this house is an older solid wood door with large windows. I recommend you upgrade this door to an ENERGY STAR?????? rated, insulated door, for our climate zone.



Rebates: By replacing your main door with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified door for our climate zone, you could potentially save $60 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.6 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

PROPANE Instantaneous Upgrade from Electric



You currently have a 189 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $1300 per year in energy costs, slightly increase your annual energy usage by 0.4 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

PROPANE FURNACE



Your house is currently heated with an 84% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $970 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 34 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. This rebate amount increases if you enter into the DHERP described above.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



WOOD STOVE



I have modeled the estimated energy savings if you were to upgrade your wood stove and fireplace to two newer wood stoves, and used them for about 50% of the heating requirement for the upper floors of the house. Getting that wood heat down into the basement could be acheived by using your furnace fan to circulate heat.



I modeled an upgrade to two new EPA qualified wood stoves that have a dedicated outside combustion air pipe and an efficiency of at least 80%. Wood stoves must meet either the Canadian Standards Association (CSA) B415.1-M92 (performance testing of solid-fuel burning appliances) or the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) wood burning appliances standards (40 CFR Part 60).



Rebates &amp; Savings: By using an 80% efficient wood stove for 50% of the heating requirements of the main living area of your house - and paying for wood delivery in Yellowknife (estimated $420/cord) - you would see approximately $0 in savings at current oil prices. Although this is surprising, it comes down to the cost of the fuel and the efficiency of your furnace vs the cost and efficiency of heating with a wood stove. Modest savings would be acheived if you self-harvest the wood. Installing an eligible wood stove qualifies for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS





LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:



        - a 685 CFM range hood   

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



